    "q-91440": {
      "dom_id": "step-q91440",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "If the speed of an object is measured in $\\text{feet/min},$ what are the units of its acceleration?",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{feet/min}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{min}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14265567/assets/2152cd701002de9a804df6fe489fecd7cbd4fd1a3878962b7cad5799c1fd5b3d.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{feet/min}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{feet}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{feet}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{feet}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-91440-a",
              "type": "math",
