    "q-26502": {
      "dom_id": "step-q26502",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "An object accelerates with constant acceleration from $0\\,\\text{m/s}$ to $20\\,\\text{m/s}$ in $50$ seconds. Find its acceleration.",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph.  To plot a speed-time graph, we need two points.\n\n- The object starts at a speed of $0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(0,0).$\n\n- In $50$ seconds, the car reaches a speed of $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(50,20).$\n\nSince the acceleration is constant, we plot the two points and draw a line through them as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14265567/assets/db091084cf48d5d7a97e07829b897d7d5d5e28a3ae656e621a4b95282046de95.png)\n\nThe slope of the object's speed-time graph gives its acceleration. The slope of this line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{20\\text{ m/s}-0\\text{ m/s}}{50\\text{ s}-0\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\text{ m/s}}{50\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the object is $0.4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
